"""openFDA DRUG API response fixtures.

Built from ACTUAL api.fda.gov payloads captured live on 2026-08-05, trimmed only
in the longest label sections (marked […]) and by dropping each submission's
`application_docs` array. Every awkward reality the parsers must survive is real
and present here, because each one was found by measurement rather than
anticipated:

  * BLA125514 (Keytruda) has a full `openfda` block — the easy case.
  * NDA017488 (Modicon) has NO `openfda` block at all, and is findable ONLY via
    products[].active_ingredients[].name. Measured live, that field is present
    on 28,904 of 29,252 applications (99%) while openfda.generic_name is present
    on 12,488 (43%) — matching on the obvious field would silently drop 57% of
    the database.
  * ANDA076290 and NDA017488 are COMBINATION products (two active ingredients
    in one product) and both are entirely Discontinued — approved once and
    withdrawn, which is not the same fact as never approved.
  * ANDA213576 (eluxadoline) has an ORIG submission with status TA, not AP:
    a TENTATIVE approval, which is NOT an approval. Measured vocabulary across
    the whole endpoint: AP 25,490, TA 1,140.
  * The enforcement record carries an EMPTY `openfda` block, which is the norm —
    only 3,169 of 17,860 drug recalls (18%) can be joined to an application.

DRUGSFDA_PAGE is ordered so results[0] is the openfda-rich case and results[2]
is the one with no openfda block, so a test can address either deliberately.
"""

